---
title: Croissants
servings: 24
yield: about 24 croissants
tags: [french, vegetarian, weekend, make-ahead]
cuisine: french
time: 4 hours 30 minutes
source: Baker's Croissants by Susan Reid, King Arthur Baking, https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/bakers-croissants-recipe
---

== Sponge ==

Crack the @eggs{2} into a measuring jug and add @water{}(warm) to make 454 g (2 cups) of liquid.

In a #stand mixer{} bowl, mix the egg and water with 1 tbsp of the @granulated sugar{50%g}, 362 g of the @all-purpose flour{660%g} and the @instant yeast{2.25%tsp}. Leave it to sponge while you make the butter block.

== Butter block ==

Beat the @unsalted butter{425%g}(cool, in 1-inch chunks), @table salt{3/4%tsp} and @all-purpose flour{60%g} with the paddle on low until smooth, without beating in air.

Shape it into an 8-inch square on plastic wrap and refrigerate ~{30%minutes}.

== Dough ==

Add the @unsalted butter{28%g}(melted) to the sponge, then the rest of the sugar, 298 g more flour, the @nonfat dry milk{56%g}(optional) and @table salt{16%g}, plus @vanilla extract{1%tsp}(optional) for a sweeter pastry.

Mix, then knead ~{5%minutes}. If it is still sticky, add flour 2 tbsp at a time, up to 60 g more. Pat it into a 9-inch square and refrigerate ~{30%minutes}.

== Laminate ==

Roll the dough to a 12-inch square. Set the butter block on it turned 45 degrees, like a diamond. Fold the dough corners in to meet in the middle, pinch the seams shut, dust with flour and turn it over.

Roll it to 20 x 10 inches and fold it in thirds like a letter. Turn it 90 degrees, roll to 20 x 10 inches again and fold in thirds. Refrigerate ~{30%minutes}.

Give it two more rolls and folds the same way, then refrigerate at least ~{1%hour}, or overnight. It can be frozen at this point.

== Shape and bake ==

Cut the dough in half and keep one half cold. Roll the other to 13 x 18 inches and trim 1/4 inch off every edge.

Cut it in thirds lengthwise, then down the middle, to make six 4 x 9-inch pieces. Cut each in half on the diagonal to make two triangles.

Stretch each triangle a little, cut a 1-inch notch in the middle of its base and roll it up from the base, finishing with the tip underneath. Curve the ends and set them on a #baking sheet{} lined with parchment. Repeat with the second half.

Cover and refrigerate ~{30%minutes}, then leave at room temperature ~{1%hour} to 1 1/2 hours, until clearly bigger and a fingertip dent stays.

Heat the oven to 425°F (220°C). Brush them with an @eggs{1} beaten with @water{1%tbsp}.

Bake ~{15%minutes}, turn the oven down to 350°F (175°C) and bake ~{10%minutes} to 15 minutes more, until deep golden. Cool on the pan on a #wire rack{} ~{20%minutes}.

> Keep the butter and dough at about the same coolness. If the butter
> softens or the dough fights back, refrigerate it 15 to 30 minutes and go on.
